A classic example at the beginning of learning programming.
`hello.c` is the source file.

@[hello.c@=
@<hello@:executable@:Language@/C@/Module@/module@>
@

Print the greeting to standard output.
@<hello@:executable@:Language@/C@/Module@/main body@=
printf("Hello World!\n");
@~ Project@/hello@/topics@/output @
The command has a single usage without options or arguments.
@<hello@:executable@:UI@/Command Line@/invocation syntax@=
{
    name: 'hello',
}
@
@<File@/hello.c@=
{"description": "C source file of the hello executable."}
@
@<Index@/Project@=
{"description": "Project-specific index entries."}
@
@<Index@/Project@/hello@=
{"description": "Index entries for the hello program."}
@
@<Index@/Project@/hello@/topics@=
{"description": "Topic markers for the hello program."}
@
@<Index@/Project@/hello@/topics@/output@=
{"description": "Code that writes the program's greeting output."}
@
@<Specialization@/executable@/hello@=
{"description": "The hello executable C module."}
@